[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Divergences found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Harness defect (found first, and it was hiding everything else)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cargo test` builds the integration-test binaries but **does not rebuild the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cdylib` artifact**. The first version of `tests/common/mod.rs` `dlopen`ed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `target/release/libcolourblind_lib.so` directly, so every differential test was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m comparing the C library against whatever Rust build happened to be on disk. A
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m deliberate mutation (`P_RG` 0.829… → 0.5, a gross error) still produced 36/36
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m passes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fix: `tests/common/mod.rs` now shells out to `cargo build --lib` for the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m detected profile before `dlopen`, and then asserts `mtime(.so) >= mtime(src/lib.rs)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so a stale load can never pass silently. Re-running the same mutation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m afterwards failed 18 of 36 tests (13 valid-path, 5 error-path), confirming the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m suite is actually sensitive.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. Real translation bug — misaligned pointer dereference (rows C16 / E10)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C compiles each `*Red` / `*Green` / `*Blue` access to a plain `movss`, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m has no alignment requirement, so the C library accepts a `float*` that is not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4-byte aligned and computes the correct result. The Rust used plain `*p`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m dereferences, which are undefined behaviour on a misaligned pointer: the release
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m build happened to work, but the **debug** build aborted with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m panicked at src/lib.rs:147:31:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m misaligned pointer dereference: address must be a multiple of 0x4 but is 0x7f98de3f8109
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m thread caused non-unwinding panic. aborting.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m That is a hard divergence — the C returns a value, the Rust kills the process.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fix: `src/lib.rs` now uses `ptr::read_unaligned` / `ptr::write_unaligned` in all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m three transforms. For aligned pointers this compiles to the identical `movss`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so there is no behaviour change on the aligned path; for unaligned pointers it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m now matches the C. Both profiles pass afterwards.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Notes on scope
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Reference C build.** The ground-truth `.so` is the one produced by the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   documented command, i.e. CMake with no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` → no `-O` flags.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   The disassembly confirms straight-line unoptimised `mulss`/`addss`/`subss`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   with no FMA contraction and no reassociation, which is what the Rust mirrors
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   operand-role for operand-role.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Constant pooling.** GCC merged several C literals that round to the same
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `f32` (`0x2000` serves both `0.17055699213417f` and `0.17055699092998f`;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0x2030` both Tritanopia red coefficients; `0x2038`/`0x203c` are shared
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   between the green and blue rows). Rust's decimal→`f32` rounding agrees, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   row C20 verifies behaviourally through both `.so`s.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Soak evidence.** `CB_SOAK=<n>` multiplies every randomized row. Wall time
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   scales linearly (`0.03s` → `0.17s` → `1.49s` at 1× / 20× / 200×), which is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   evidence the loops really run; at 200× the suite performs on the order of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   10^8 paired C/Rust calls with zero bit differences.
